; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED. See weave4.task for the shared design -- the spool fixture, the seat
; geometry and the 8.25 mm it is pinned to, the worst_of grouping and the zero
; idle floor. What is new here is the two ENDS.
;
; A TERMINATED RUN. Every other document in this family asks the cable to be
; woven and stops there: the rod begins wherever the scene laid it and ends
; wherever the last crossing left it. A real run does neither. It starts at
; something that holds it and finishes at something it has to be got into, and
; those two facts are most of what makes routing a cable different from bending
; one.
;
; So this variant adds a fitting at each end. The ANCHOR marks where the run
; begins -- the end the support hand takes, which the scene was already doing
; and now says out loud. The PORT is the destination, and it is a GOAL CLAUSE
; rather than scenery: the route is not finished until the free end is inside
; it.
;
; WHAT THE ANCHOR IS. A fastening, and it did not used to be. This document said
; the opposite for a release: "It is a stop, not a fastening. Nothing in this
; scene welds the rod to that block, so what actually holds the far end is still
; the support arm." The rod's spec declares `anchors`, this variant sets it, and
; the first capsule is welded to the world where it was built -- so the caution
; outlived the thing it was cautioning about, which is the worse of the two ways
; a document can be wrong about its own scene.
;
; The bracket is drawn as what it now does: two rails either side of the cable,
; a hood the run terminates under, and a saddle across it. Nothing in the
; assembly sits UNDER the cable -- a mounting plate laid across the rod's path
; puts a welded body inside a static box, which is a contact with no answer --
; so it straddles, which is also what a real saddle does. See
; assets/terminals.py.
;
; The support arm is still a second hand on the run. What it is no longer is the
; only thing holding the far end, and it now takes the rod 20 mm in from its own
; tip rather than at the constant the 0.50 m family was written with -- see
; weave_anchor_y, which is what stops the two hands standing in one place.
;
; TWO STATIONS, not four. The question this document exists to ask is about the
; two ends; how a long weave behaves between them is weave4's question and it is
; already answered. Two crossings is the shortest route that still alternates,
; and at 12.2 degrees of turning it is the shallowest board in the suite -- which
; is the point: nothing here should be hard except the ends.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem ROUTE_THE_CABLE_FROM_THE_ANCHOR_INTO_THE_PORT)

  (:suite cable_route)

  (:language weave the cable between the two pegs and route its free end into the port)

  (:staging
    (:cameras cable_port2@v1)
    ; Two crossings and a plug, on weave4's per-crossing budget.
    (:max-steps 6000)
    ; The rod's shape and placement, drawn per episode; the board does not move.
    ; The anchor bracket DOES, and has to: the shift and the yaw both move the
    ; end that is welded to the world, and a plate 32 mm across left at a fixed
    ; pose is one the run visibly does not start at. It is placed from the rod's
    ; own drawn tip through weave_terminals -- the same function that places the
    ; declared pose -- so the two are one arithmetic over different inputs.
    (:episodes (:count 6) (:seed 0) (:sampler staging:CABLE_BOW))
  )

  (:objects
    cable - port2_cable
  )

  (:fixtures
    peg0   - port2_peg0
    peg1   - port2_peg1
    ; Both ends of the run. Declared as fixtures because they are THERE -- the
    ; rod can foul either, and a fitting the physics carries but the document
    ; does not name is a fitting that silently changes the task. The two are
    ; one asset (`cable_block`, one 32 x 32 mm footprint) realised by two
    ; entities: a bracket that clamps and a socket that receives.
    anchor - port2_anchor
    port   - port2_port
  )

  ; Sides -1, +1: one crossing each way, the shortest route that alternates.
  ; The seat boxes are weave4's, unchanged.
  ;
  ; The PORT box is the same 45 mm reach as a seat, measured off the fitting
  ; instead of a post, and open on the side the run arrives from. A station and
  ; a port ask the same question of different fixtures -- is the rod where this
  ; thing wants it -- and scoring them against two different tolerances would
  ; make the number say which clause failed rather than how well the route ran.
  (:regions
    (seat0    (:target peg0) (:ranges ((-0.045 -0.035 -0.003 0.035))))
    (seat1    (:target peg1) (:ranges (( 0.003 -0.035  0.045 0.035))))
    (portbox  (:target port) (:ranges ((-0.045 -0.045  0.045 0.045))))
  )

  ; No init constraint: both arms start at the embodiment's own home, touching
  ; nothing. Taking the far end is a policy's first move.
  (:init)

  ; Three clauses under one worst_of, so each station and the port are a third
  ; of the route each. Idle is still 0: at t = 0 the rod lies on the robot's
  ; side of the station line with its free end nowhere near the socket, so no
  ; clause is satisfied by the scene arriving.
  ;
  ; AGAINST THE BOW ALONE. This layout's near row stands at x = 0.5200, so its
  ; near-side seat boxes begin at 0.4750 and a rod bowed 20 mm off a 0.42 spawn
  ; reaches 0.440, clear by 35.0 mm. Against the WHOLE spawn envelope -- bow,
  ; second harmonic and shift, all at an extreme and aligned -- the worst
  ; capsule reaches 0.480 and is 5.0 mm INSIDE that box edge, and
  ; `staging:CABLE_BOW` has no clearance guard the way `WEAVE_RIGID` does. What
  ; that envelope is and is not is written up on WEAVE_CABLE_WAVE in
  ; assets/cable.py; eleven of the thirteen layouts are inside it and no
  ; rollout has measured an idle score at these constants.
  ;
  ; The port asks for 0.071 of the rod where a seat asks 0.047 -- on this 68
  ; capsule rod, about five bodies against three. A seat holds the cable
  ; PASSING, so a span abreast of the post is the whole of what it means; a port
  ; is where the run TERMINATES, and an end that has only just broken the plane
  ; is not plugged in.
  (:goal
    (worst_of
      (And
        (>= (containment cable seat0) 0.047)
        (>= (containment cable seat1) 0.047)
        (>= (containment cable portbox) 0.071)
      )
      (And
        (> (tcp-distance cable) 0.05)
        (<= (speed cable) 0.05)
      )
    )
  )

  (:observe
    (once (<= (tcp-distance cable) 0.05))
    (straightness cable)
  )

  ; The same graphs the weaves run. Each reads the stations and the rod -- from
  ; the simulator on `gt`, from the `cable` camera on `vision` -- and works them
  ; in turn; the port is one more station to them, which is the point of
  ; terminating the run with a fixture the perception can already see rather
  ; than with a special case.
  (:artifacts
    (:routine "routine:RoutineWeave")
    (:graph gt "graphs/weave")
    (:graph vision "graphs/weave_vision")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier authored)
    (:source "AUTHORED -- no upstream. weave4's line shortened to two stations, with a
              terminal fitting added at each end of the run.")
    (:note "The reference cable scenes end at a peg line; a real run starts at something that
            holds it and finishes at something it is got into. The anchor is a bracket the rod
            is WELDED to (RodSpec.anchors, at the first capsule's own build pose), and the
            port is a goal clause rather than scenery. Both are drawn as fittings rather than
            as boxes: EntityCableTerminal straddles the cable with a saddle, EntityCablePort is
            an open channel with a stop at the back. Two F1 spools 155 mm apart at x = 0.52,
            12.2 deg of accumulated turning (L1). The rod is 0.82 m at 68 capsules; the whole
            route -- anchor to two seats to the port mouth -- measures 593 mm, so 227 mm is
            left over. The port stands 75 mm past the last station and 61 mm out in x, a 43 deg
            lead-out; see WEAVE_PORT_STANDOFF for the four standoffs that angle was measured
            over.")
  )
)
